---

## Test Smell Audit — `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`

**Framework:** MSTest 4.1.0 · **.NET 10** · **Integration test** (`[TestCategory("Integration")]`)

### Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High | 3 | 3 |
| Medium | 1 | 1 |
| Low | 0 | 0 |
| **Total** | **4** | **4** |

---

### High-Severity Findings

#### ① Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test) — `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (line 113)

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
}
```

**Problem:** Exercises code but never asserts. MSTest marks it green as long as no exception is thrown — the DB could silently drop every row and this test still passes.

**Fix:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — 100 % coverage, 0 % verification.

---

#### ② Conditional Test Logic — `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (line 94)

```csharp
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

**Problem:** The `if`/`else` means one branch is never tested for a given execution. If `loaded` is never a `PremiumUser`, the discount-rate check silently never runs — a hidden coverage gap.

**Fix — split into two focused tests:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_StandardUser_ReturnsCorrectName()
{
    _repository.Insert(new User("eve@example.com", "Eve"));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");
    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_PremiumUser_HasPositiveDiscountRate()
{
    _repository.Insert(new PremiumUser("vip@example.com", "Vip", discountRate: 0.1m));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("vip@example.com") as PremiumUser;
    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Whichever branch never fires is dead code — the test gives an illusion of type-coverage.

---

#### ③ Sleepy Test — `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 82)

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(3000);
```

**Problem:** A hard 3-second wall-clock delay on every run. Even in integration tests, `Thread.Sleep` is a smell: it makes the suite slow **and** flaky (the real operation may take longer under load, or finish in 10 ms on fast hardware).

**Integration calibration note:** Integration tests sometimes need to wait for async side-effects, but the correct pattern is a polling/retry loop with a timeout, not a fixed sleep.

**Fix:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    // Poll with a sensible timeout instead of sleeping
    var timeout = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
    var interval = TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(100);
    var sw = Stopwatch.StartNew();
    while (sw.Elapsed < timeout)
    {
        if (_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id))
            break;
        await Task.Delay(interval);
    }

    Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id),
        "Notification was not sent within the timeout period.");
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Adds 3 s to every CI run (compounds across suites) and is non-deterministic — will eventually produce a flaky failure.

---

### Medium-Severity Findings

#### ④ Assertion Roulette — `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (line 28)

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
```

**Problem:** Four assertions, zero message parameters. When one fails in CI, the failure log shows `Assert.AreEqual failed. Expected:<Alice>. Actual:<null>` with no explanation of which semantic property was wrong or why.

**Fix — add message parameters:**
```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should persist unchanged");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should persist unchanged");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Auto-generated ID should be positive");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slower triage when failures occur in CI — developers must read source to identify which property mismatched.

---

### Smells Checked But Not Found (Clean)

| Smell (Academic Name) | Result |
|---|---|
| **Mystery Guest** | ✅ Clean — in-memory SQLite (`:memory:`) is hermetic; no real filesystem/network/env dependency |
| **Eager Test** | ✅ Clean — each test calls ≤ 3 production methods, appropriate for integration scope |
| **Magic Number Test** | ✅ Clean — `3` (line 76) and `10` (line 132) are contextually self-documenting (N items inserted, then count asserted) |
| **Sensitive Equality** | ✅ Clean — no `ToString()` comparisons |
| **Exception Handling** | ✅ Clean — no `try`/`catch` blocks in tests |
| **General Fixture** | ✅ Clean — `_connection` and `_repository` are used by all 8 tests |
| **Ignored / Disabled Test** | ✅ Clean — no `[Ignore]` attributes |
| **Duplicate Assert** | ✅ Clean — no identical assertion repeated |
| **Lazy Test** | ✅ Clean — each test has a distinct action despite sharing `Insert` for setup |
| **Constructor Initialization** | ✅ Clean — uses `[TestInitialize]` properly |
| **Default Test** | ✅ Clean — class is meaningfully named |
| **Redundant Print** | ✅ Clean — no `Console.WriteLine` |
| **Redundant Assertion** | ✅ Clean — no tautological assertions |
| **Resource Optimism** | ✅ Clean — in-memory DB is always available |
| **Empty Test** | ✅ Clean — all methods have executable statements |

---

### Well-Written Tests Worth Noting

- **`DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase`** — textbook: single action, single negative assertion, clear naming.
- **`InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity`** — good async pattern, correct use of `Task.WhenAll`, and meaningful assertion on final state.
- **`ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted`** — good use of `CollectionAssert.AllItemsAreNotNull` beyond a simple count check.

---

### Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Test | Effort | Why First |
|----------|-------|------|--------|-----------|
| **1** | Assertion-Free Test | `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` | 2 min | Prevents a false-pass — highest risk |
| **2** | Conditional Test Logic | `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` | 10 min | Eliminates a hidden coverage gap |
| **3** | Sleepy Test | `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` | 10 min | Removes 3 s of wall-clock and flake risk |
| **4** | Assertion Roulette | `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` | 2 min | Low-effort quality-of-life for CI triage |

**Bottom line:** 4 of 8 tests are clean. The 4 findings are real but tractable — the highest-impact fix (`BulkInsert`) is a two-minute, one-line addition. The suite's use of in-memory SQLite and proper `[TestInitialize]`/`[TestCleanup]` lifecycle is solid; the integration-scope patterns (multi-method calls, count assertions) are appropriate and were **not** penalized.
